# TypeScript Typing Standards

## 1. No Type Inference — Explicit Types Everywhere

**Every const, let, assertion, initialization, or declaration must have an explicit type.** Do not rely on TypeScript inference.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inference
const items = getItems();
const { result } = renderHook(() => useMyHook());
const handler = (e: Event) => e.preventDefault();

// ✅ GOOD — explicit types
const items: Item[] = getItems();
const { result }: RenderHookResult<MyHookReturn, void> = renderHook(() => useMyHook());
const handler: (e: Event) => void = (e: Event): void => e.preventDefault();
```

Use type aliases for complex or repeated types (e.g. `RenderHookResult`, summary shapes).

## 2. No Double Assertion

**Never use `as unknown as T`** (double assertion) at call sites. Encapsulate in typed helpers when needed.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — double assertion at call site
const mock: MockType = value as unknown as MockType;
Object.defineProperty(obj, 'prop', { value: mock as unknown as DomType });

// ✅ GOOD — typed module with helper (single assertion)
interface MockedModule {
  readonly fn: MockType;
}
function getMockedModule(module: typeof ImportedModule): MockedModule {
  // @ts-expect-error - jest.mock replaces at runtime; types differ
  return module as MockedModule;
}
const module: MockedModule = getMockedModule(ImportedModule);
const mock: MockType = module.fn;

// ✅ GOOD — typed helper for mock-to-DOM
function toDomType(mock: MockType): DomType {
  // @ts-expect-error - Mock is partial; satisfies runtime usage
  return mock as DomType;
}
Object.defineProperty(obj, 'prop', { value: toDomType(mock) });
```

## 3. When Assertion Is Required

- Use **one** assertion per conversion; use `@ts-expect-error` when types don't overlap.
- Prefer **typed helpers** that encapsulate the assertion.
- Never use double assertion (`as unknown as T`) in the main code flow.

## 4. Complex Objects

Use interfaces, type aliases, and generics. Prefer `interface` for object shapes and `type` for unions/intersections.
